wasm jit: direct calls to the runtime's own InternalCalls, handles included; string ctors (gex)

0009 called guest InternalCalls and P/Invokes straight from the residual's scratch. The runtime's
own InternalCalls (functions of this wasm module: GCHandle.InternalGet, RuntimeType.GetName,
Array.GetLowerBoundInternal, FastAllocateString, Monitor, Enum, reflection handles) still went
through interp_entry and the managed-to-native wrapper (Hollow Knight hg10: 3.8M "icall-handles"
and part of 13.2M "icall" interpreter legs a run). mono_wasm_jit_gex_native_site now builds a
`mono` site for them: the wrapper's work is redone in C (null check of `this`; for a handles icall
each reference argument becomes a pointer to a local of the C frame, out/in-out references go
through a local and back with a write barrier; the GC-safe transition for a foreign icall; the
interruption checkpoint that throws a pending exception), and the call goes through the
interpreter's own m2n trampoline for the wasm signature, probed at compile time with the new
mono_wasm_try_get_interp_to_native_trampoline. Struct returns, generic methods and float-bearing
scalar structs keep the residual. GEXMONO_WASM_JIT_MONO_ICALLS=0 turns it off.

`new string (...)` under the wasm JIT now calls the managed static String.Ctor overload the
wrapper redirects to, as an ordinary managed call.

Embed suite at threshold 1: interpreter-routed residual calls 70,359 -> 236 (icall 39,966 and
icall-handles 30,659 -> 0). Test: T.MonoIcalls (handles, out handles, an exception raised inside an
icall, string ctors), checked against CoreCLR.

